Chapter 1 of the course. Everything else builds on getting this one model right.

## The mechanism

A re-render starts in exactly one place: **a component's own state changes.** From there React re-renders that component and, by default, **every component below it in the tree** — regardless of whether their props changed, regardless of whether they use the state.

```javascript
function App() {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setOpen(true)}>Open</button>
      <VerySlowComponent />   {/* re-renders on every toggle */}
      <AnotherSlowThing />    {/* this one too */}
    </div>
  );
}
```

Neither slow component receives `open`. Neither cares about it. Both re-render on every click, because their parent did.

## The myth

> "A component re-renders when its props change."

This is backwards, and it's the most consequential misconception in React performance work.

Props changing is a **symptom**, not a cause. A child receives new props precisely *because* the parent re-rendered — the parent re-rendering is the event, and the new props arrive as a consequence. Change a child's props without re-rendering the parent and nothing happens, because that situation cannot occur.

The one case where props matter is when a component is wrapped in `React.memo` — then, and only then, does React compare props to decide whether to skip. Without `memo`, props are irrelevant to the re-render decision.

## Fix 1: move state down

If the state is only used by a small part of the tree, push it into a component that wraps only that part.

```javascript
// Before — `open` lives in App, so everything re-renders
function App() {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setOpen(true)}>Open</button>
      {open && <Modal onClose={() => setOpen(false)} />}
      <VerySlowComponent />
    </div>
  );
}

// After — state is isolated; VerySlowComponent is outside its subtree
function ModalTrigger() {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setOpen(true)}>Open</button>
      {open && <Modal onClose={() => setOpen(false)} />}
    </>
  );
}

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <ModalTrigger />
      <VerySlowComponent />   {/* now unaffected */}
    </div>
  );
}
```

No `memo`, no `useCallback`, no dependency arrays. The state simply no longer sits above the expensive thing. **Prefer this over memoization whenever the state is genuinely local** — it has no comparison cost and nothing to keep in sync.

## Fix 2: children as props

When the expensive component *must* stay inside the stateful one, you can still keep it out of the re-render by passing it in from above. That's [entry 2](/advanced-react/2), and it's the technique most people never learn.

## The custom-hook trap

State inside a custom hook is still state in the component that calls it.

```javascript
function useModal() {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  return { open, setOpen };
}

function App() {
  const { open, setOpen } = useModal();  // App re-renders on every setOpen
  return <><VerySlowComponent /> ...</>;
}
```

The hook hides the `useState` call from view, but not from React. Worse, hooks compose: a hook that calls another hook that owns state will re-render the host component every time that inner state changes — and nothing at the call site hints at it. When profiling an unexplained re-render, **read into the custom hooks**, not just the component body.

## Related

- [Elements, Children as Props, and Re-renders](/advanced-react/2) — the fix when you can't move state down
- [Memoization](/advanced-react/5)
- [What Actually Triggers a Re-render](/react-guide/14)
- [Where State Should Live](/react-guide/26)

Chapter 2, and the highest-leverage idea in the course. It turns on a distinction most React developers never make explicit: **an element is not a component.**

## Element vs component

A **component** is a function. An **element** is a plain object describing what to render.

```javascript
// Component — a function
function Sidebar() {
  return <nav>...</nav>;
}

// Element — an object, produced by JSX
const element = <Sidebar />;

// What JSX compiles to:
const element = React.createElement(Sidebar, null);

// What that returns — roughly:
{
  type: Sidebar,        // a reference to the function
  props: {},
  key: null,
  // ...internals
}
```

Writing `<Sidebar />` **does not call `Sidebar`.** It creates a small object that says "when you get around to it, render this function." React calls it later, during reconciliation.

## The bail-out rule

During reconciliation React compares the previous element at a position with the next one. If they are **the same object by reference**, React knows nothing about that subtree can have changed, and skips re-rendering it entirely.

That's the whole trick. The question becomes: *how do I make the element reference stay the same across a re-render?*

Answer: have it created somewhere that isn't re-rendering.

## Children as props

`children` is an ordinary prop. This:

```javascript
<Wrapper>
  <VerySlowComponent />
</Wrapper>
```

is exactly this:

```javascript
<Wrapper children={<VerySlowComponent />} />
```

And critically — **the `<VerySlowComponent />` element is created in the scope of whoever wrote that JSX**, not inside `Wrapper`. So when `Wrapper`'s own state changes and `Wrapper` re-renders, `props.children` is still the *same object* it was before. React compares, sees an identical reference, and skips the entire subtree.

## Before and after

```javascript
// BEFORE — the element is created inside the stateful component,
// so it's a brand-new object on every render. Everything re-renders.
function ScrollContainer() {
  const [scrollY, setScrollY] = useState(0);

  return (
    <div onScroll={(e) => setScrollY(e.target.scrollTop)}>
      <VerySlowComponent />
    </div>
  );
}
```

```javascript
// AFTER — the element is created in App and handed down.
// ScrollContainer re-renders freely; children does not.
function ScrollContainer({ children }) {
  const [scrollY, setScrollY] = useState(0);

  return (
    <div onScroll={(e) => setScrollY(e.target.scrollTop)}>
      {children}
    </div>
  );
}

function App() {
  return (
    <ScrollContainer>
      <VerySlowComponent />
    </ScrollContainer>
  );
}
```

The scroll handler fires sixty times a second, `ScrollContainer` re-renders sixty times a second, and `VerySlowComponent` renders **once**. No `memo`, no `useCallback`, no dependency array to maintain.

## Why this beats memoization

| | `React.memo` | children as props |
|---|---|---|
| Cost per render | Shallow prop comparison | None — a reference check React already does |
| Breaks when | Any prop is a new object/array/arrow | It doesn't; there are no props to compare |
| Maintenance | Every new prop must stay referentially stable | Nothing to maintain |
| Visible at call site | No | Yes — the structure *is* the optimization |

Memoization is a patch applied against React's default behaviour. This is a restructuring that makes the default behaviour do what you want.

## The limit

`children` stays stable only while the component **holding** the JSX doesn't re-render. If `App` itself re-renders, it recreates the `<VerySlowComponent />` element and the bail-out is lost. The technique moves the problem up the tree — so put the state as low as possible first ([entry 1](/advanced-react/1)), then use this for what remains.

Note also that this is why `React.memo` often disappoints: passing JSX children to a memoized component defeats it, because `children` is a fresh element object on every parent render.

## Related

- [Intro to Re-renders](/advanced-react/1)
- [Elements as Props: Configuration Without Re-renders](/advanced-react/3) — the same idea as named props
- [Memoization](/advanced-react/5)
- [Elements → Fiber → DOM](/react-guide/2)

Chapter 3. `children` is just a prop — so the [entry 2](/advanced-react/2) bail-out works through *any* prop, not only `children`. That turns it into a general configuration pattern.

## The pattern

```javascript
function Layout({ header, sidebar, footer, children }) {
  const [collapsed, setCollapsed] = useState(false);

  return (
    <div className={collapsed ? 'layout collapsed' : 'layout'}>
      <header>{header}</header>
      {!collapsed && <aside>{sidebar}</aside>}
      <main>{children}</main>
      <footer>{footer}</footer>
    </div>
  );
}

function App() {
  return (
    <Layout
      header={<Header />}
      sidebar={<ExpensiveNav />}
      footer={<Footer />}
    >
      <Dashboard />
    </Layout>
  );
}
```

Toggling `collapsed` re-renders `Layout` and nothing else. Every one of those elements was created in `App`, so all four references survive.

## Why it beats a config object

The alternative is passing data and letting the component build the UI:

```javascript
// Configuration by data — the component must know every variant
<Button iconName="trash" iconColor="red" iconSize="large" />
```

```javascript
// Configuration by element — the caller supplies the UI
<Button icon={<TrashIcon color="red" size="large" />} />
```

The first grows a prop for every option any caller ever needs, and the component accumulates branches. The second is open-ended: the caller passes whatever element they like, and `Button` never changes. This is the escape from prop explosion in design-system components.

## Conditional element props are cheap

A common worry: doesn't `sidebar={<ExpensiveNav />}` do the expensive work even when collapsed?

No. Creating the element is just allocating a small object — `ExpensiveNav` is **not called**. The function only runs if React actually renders that element. So this is fine:

```javascript
<Layout sidebar={<ExpensiveNav />} />
// ...and inside Layout:
{!collapsed && <aside>{sidebar}</aside>}   // ExpensiveNav never runs while collapsed
```

The cost of an unrendered element prop is one object allocation. Not a render.

## Default values

Because an element prop is just a value, defaults are ordinary JavaScript:

```javascript
function Button({ icon = <DefaultIcon />, children }) {
  return <button>{icon}{children}</button>;
}
```

If you need to *modify* a passed element — inject a prop the parent shouldn't have to know about — use `cloneElement`:

```javascript
function Button({ icon, size }) {
  const sizedIcon = icon ? cloneElement(icon, { size }) : null;
  return <button>{sizedIcon}</button>;
}
```

Use it sparingly. `cloneElement` silently overrides props the caller set, which makes behaviour hard to trace. When you find yourself reaching for it repeatedly, the caller probably wants a [render prop](/advanced-react/4) or context instead.

## Related

- [Elements, Children as Props, and Re-renders](/advanced-react/2)
- [Render Props](/advanced-react/4) — for when the element needs data the parent owns
- [Compound Components](/react-guide/21)

Chapter 4. [Elements as props](/advanced-react/3) is a great configuration tool right up to the point where the passed-in UI needs something the *receiving* component owns. Then it breaks, in two specific ways.

## The two problems elements-as-props can't solve

**Problem 1: the element can't see the component's state.**

Say a `Button` tracks hover and you want the caller's icon to react to it. The icon element was created by the caller, before `Button` ever ran — there is no way to hand it `isHovered` after the fact.

**Problem 2: injecting props couples you to one API.**

The `cloneElement` workaround from [entry 3](/advanced-react/3) has a hidden cost. If `Button` injects `size` into whatever icon it's given:

```javascript
const sizedIcon = cloneElement(icon, { size: buttonSize });
```

…then it has silently declared that every icon in the world accepts a `size` prop. Swap in an icon library that calls it `fontSize` and the defaults stop applying — **with no error and no warning.** The button dictates the icon's API, which is exactly backwards.

## The fix: pass a function instead of an element

A render prop is a function that returns an element. The difference from a component is only *who calls it*: React calls components; **you call render props yourself**, which means you choose what to pass in.

```javascript
function Button({ renderIcon, size, appearance, children }) {
  const [isHovered, setIsHovered] = useState(false);

  // We decide what the caller gets to see.
  const iconParams = {
    size: size === 'large' ? 'lg' : 'sm',
    color: appearance === 'primary' ? 'white' : 'black',
    isHovered,
  };

  return (
    <button
      onMouseEnter={() => setIsHovered(true)}
      onMouseLeave={() => setIsHovered(false)}
    >
      {renderIcon(iconParams)}
      {children}
    </button>
  );
}
```

Now the call site is in charge, and both problems disappear:

```javascript
// Defaults, spread as-is
<Button renderIcon={(params) => <TrashIcon {...params} />}>Delete</Button>

// Override one of them
<Button renderIcon={(params) => <TrashIcon {...params} color="red" />}>Delete</Button>

// Adapt to an icon with a completely different API — no cloneElement,
// and Button never had to know this library exists.
<Button renderIcon={({ size, isHovered }) => (
  <OtherIcon fontSize={size === 'lg' ? 24 : 16} highlighted={isHovered} />
)}>
  Delete
</Button>
```

The data flow is a little unusual to read, but it's **explicit and traceable** — no invisible prop injection happening inside the component.

## Children as a render prop

`children` is a prop, so it can be a function too:

```javascript
function ResizeDetector({ children }) {
  const [width, setWidth] = useState(window.innerWidth);

  useEffect(() => {
    const onResize = () => setWidth(window.innerWidth);
    window.addEventListener('resize', onResize);
    return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', onResize);
  }, []);

  return children(width);
}

<ResizeDetector>
  {(width) => (width > 900 ? <DesktopNav /> : <MobileNav />)}
</ResizeDetector>
```

This was the standard way to share stateful logic before hooks, and you'll find it throughout older codebases — form libraries especially.

## But don't write this one today

For sharing *logic*, hooks replaced render props almost entirely, and rightly so:

```javascript
function useResizeDetector() {
  const [width, setWidth] = useState(window.innerWidth);
  useEffect(() => {
    const onResize = () => setWidth(window.innerWidth);
    window.addEventListener('resize', onResize);
    return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', onResize);
  }, []);
  return width;
}

const width = useResizeDetector();   // no wrapper, no nesting, no function-as-child
```

Less code, no extra component in the tree, and it composes.

## Where render props still earn their place

**1. Configuration and flexibility** — the `Button`/icon case above. Hooks don't help here at all; this is about who controls the rendered output.

**2. Logic tied to a DOM element you don't own.** A hook can subscribe to `window`, but tracking scroll *inside a specific div* means the listener has to go on that div — and the div is rendered by the component, not the caller:

```javascript
function ScrollDetector({ children }) {
  const [scroll, setScroll] = useState(0);

  return (
    <div onScroll={(e) => setScroll(e.currentTarget.scrollTop)}>
      {children(scroll)}
    </div>
  );
}
```

Doing this with a hook means handing back a ref and hoping the caller attaches it. The render prop just works.

**3. Legacy code.** Large or older codebases use this pattern heavily. You need to read it even if you never write it.

## The performance cost

This is the important trade, and it's easy to miss.

A render prop **destroys the [children-as-props](/advanced-react/2) optimization.** That technique works because the children element is created once by the parent and keeps its reference. A render prop isn't an element — it's a function React must call on every render, producing brand-new elements every time.

So the moment you convert `children` from an element into a function, everything inside it re-renders whenever the wrapper's state changes. Composition can no longer save you here, and this is precisely the point where [memoization](/advanced-react/5) becomes the actual answer.

## Decision rule

| Need | Use |
|---|---|
| Share stateful logic | Custom hook |
| Pass static UI into a component | [Element as prop](/advanced-react/3) |
| Passed UI needs the component's internal state | Render prop |
| Logic tied to a DOM node the component renders | Render prop |
| Keep the passed UI from re-rendering | [Children as props](/advanced-react/2) — *not* a render prop |

## Related

- [Elements as Props](/advanced-react/3) · [Memoization](/advanced-react/5)
- [Higher-Order Components](/advanced-react/7)
- [Render Props](/react-guide/20) · [Custom Hooks vs. Plain Helper Functions](/react-guide/13)

Chapter 7. An HOC is a function that takes a component, does something, and returns a new component that renders the original.

```javascript
function withTheme(Component) {
  return function WithTheme(props) {
    const theme = localStorage.getItem('theme');
    return <Component {...props} theme={theme} />;
  };
}

const ThemedButton = withTheme(Button);
```

Before hooks this was *the* code-reuse pattern, so any codebase more than a few years old is full of them. You almost certainly shouldn't introduce new ones — a `useTheme` hook does the above better. But three jobs still have no clean hook equivalent.

## Use case 1: enhancing callbacks

Suppose you need click analytics on every button, dropdown and link in the app.

With hooks you'd visit every component, find what's attached to `onClick`, and edit each callback. Fine for two components, miserable for two hundred — and putting the analytics call inside `Button` itself mixes concerns and forces the data to arrive as props.

An HOC wraps the callback instead:

```javascript
function withLoggedClick(Component, eventName) {
  return function WithLoggedClick({ onClick, ...props }) {
    const handleClick = (event) => {
      sendAnalytics(eventName);
      onClick?.(event);          // still fire whatever the caller passed
    };

    return <Component {...props} onClick={handleClick} />;
  };
}

const TrackedButton = withLoggedClick(Button, 'button-click');
```

Feature code keeps using `onClick` exactly as before and never learns analytics exist. Add a prop to distinguish instances and it scales:

```javascript
function withLoggedClick(Component) {
  return function WithLoggedClick({ onClick, logLocation, ...props }) {
    const handleClick = (event) => {
      sendAnalytics({ location: logLocation });
      onClick?.(event);
    };
    return <Component {...props} onClick={handleClick} />;
  };
}

<TrackedButton logLocation="navbar" onClick={save}>Save</TrackedButton>
```

## Use case 2: lifecycle tracking

The same trick for mounts and renders — attach it once, apply it to anything:

```javascript
function withMountLogging(Component, name) {
  return function WithMountLogging(props) {
    useEffect(() => {
      sendAnalytics({ event: 'mounted', name });
    }, []);
    return <Component {...props} />;
  };
}
```

## Use case 3: intercepting DOM events

Global keyboard shortcuts on `window`, which dropdowns and modals need to suppress while they're open. Rather than sprinkling `stopPropagation` through every such component:

```javascript
function withSuppressedShortcuts(Component) {
  return function WithSuppressedShortcuts(props) {
    return (
      <div onKeyDown={(e) => e.stopPropagation()}>
        <Component {...props} />
      </div>
    );
  };
}
```

Any component that should block the global shortcuts gets wrapped, and nothing inside it changes. This is the general shape hooks can't reach: **a hook runs inside a component and cannot wrap that component's output.**

## Writing one correctly

Three things the naive version gets wrong:

```javascript
function withLogging(Component) {
  function WithLogging(props) {
    // 1. Forward ALL props. Swallowing unknown props breaks every caller
    //    who passes something you didn't anticipate.
    return <Component {...props} />;
  }

  // 2. Set displayName, or DevTools shows a tree of anonymous wrappers
  //    and profiling becomes unreadable.
  WithLogging.displayName =
    `withLogging(${Component.displayName || Component.name || 'Component'})`;

  return WithLogging;
}
```

**3. Forward refs.** A ref passed to the wrapper lands on the wrapper, so it silently does nothing. Pre-React-19 you need `forwardRef`; in React 19 `ref` is an ordinary prop and passes through with the spread.

Static methods aren't copied to the wrapper either — libraries use `hoist-non-react-statics` for this.

## The rule that bites hardest

**Never create an HOC during render.**

```javascript
function App() {
  const Enhanced = withLogging(Profile);   // new function identity every render
  return <Enhanced />;
}
```

New function identity means a new element `type`, which by [the reconciliation rule](/advanced-react/6) means unmount and remount on every render — losing all state, refiring every effect, dropping focus. Call HOCs once, at module scope. This is the same bug as declaring a component inside a component.

## The prop-collision hazard

HOCs inject props by name. Two HOCs that both inject `data`, or one that injects a prop the caller also passes, silently overwrite each other — and the ordering of `{...props}` versus the injected prop decides the winner. Namespace injected props, or use a hook.

## Related

- [Render Props](/advanced-react/4) · [Diffing and Reconciliation](/advanced-react/6)
- [Higher-Order Components](/react-guide/19) · [Refs](/advanced-react/9)

Chapter 8. The problem and the four standard fixes are in [React Guide entry 28](/react-guide/28) and [Preventing Context Re-renders](/react-learnings/15). This entry adds what those don't cover.

## Recap in one line

Context has **no selective subscription**. Every consumer of a context re-renders when the provider's value changes — even a consumer that only reads one unchanging field of it.

## Addition 1: `React.memo` does not stop a context re-render

This surprises people, and it's rarely stated:

```javascript
const Child = React.memo(function Child() {
  const { theme } = useContext(ThemeContext);   // subscribes directly
  return <div className={theme}>...</div>;
});
```

`memo` compares **props**. Context is not a prop — `useContext` subscribes the component to the provider directly, bypassing the props path entirely. When the context value changes, this component re-renders no matter what `memo` says.

`memo` only helps for the components *between* the provider and the consumer, by stopping the re-render cascade from above. The consumer itself is always re-rendered.

## Addition 2: a selector on top of Context

The standard fixes (split providers, memoize the value, split state from dispatch) all reduce *how often* the value changes. None of them let a consumer say "wake me only when `user.name` changes."

You can build that with `useSyncExternalStore`, which is designed exactly for subscribing to an external store with a selector:

```javascript
function createStore(initialState) {
  let state = initialState;
  const listeners = new Set();

  return {
    getState: () => state,
    setState: (partial) => {
      state = { ...state, ...partial };
      listeners.forEach((l) => l());
    },
    subscribe: (listener) => {
      listeners.add(listener);
      return () => listeners.delete(listener);
    },
  };
}

const StoreContext = createContext(null);

function useStoreSelector(selector) {
  const store = useContext(StoreContext);
  return useSyncExternalStore(
    store.subscribe,
    () => selector(store.getState()),
    () => selector(store.getState())   // server snapshot
  );
}

// Re-renders ONLY when user.name changes.
function UserName() {
  const name = useStoreSelector((s) => s.user.name);
  return <span>{name}</span>;
}
```

The context now carries a **stable store object** that never changes identity, so the context value itself never triggers a re-render. Subscriptions and selection do the work.

One caveat: the selector must return a **referentially stable** value. Returning `{ name, email }` creates a new object on every check and re-renders every time — select primitives, or use a store library that supports shallow comparison.

## Addition 3: when to stop and reach for a library

The code above is roughly what Zustand, Jotai, and Redux's `useSelector` already do, with edge cases handled — tearing under concurrent rendering, shallow comparison, devtools, middleware.

Rule of thumb: **Context is a dependency-injection mechanism, not a state manager.** It excels at values that rarely change — theme, locale, the current user, a store handle. For state that changes frequently, or where consumers need to read narrow slices, use a store with selectors and pass the store *through* context.

## Related

- [Context Re-renders — The Problem and the Fixes](/react-guide/28)
- [Intro to Re-renders](/advanced-react/1) · [Memoization](/advanced-react/5)
- [Where State Should Live](/react-guide/26)

Chapter 9. [React Guide entry 10](/react-guide/10) covers the two basic jobs. This entry is the parts that trip people up.

## What a ref actually is

`useRef` returns the **same mutable object** on every render — a box with a `current` property. That's the whole implementation:

```javascript
const ref = useRef(initialValue);   // always the same { current: initialValue } object
```

Two consequences follow from "same object, mutable":

- **Writing to `ref.current` never schedules a render.** React has no idea it happened.
- **Reading `ref.current` during render is unsafe.** The value can differ between the render pass and the commit, which breaks under concurrent rendering. Read refs in effects and event handlers, not in render.

## Ref as an instance variable

Any value that must survive re-renders but must *not* trigger them:

```javascript
const timerId = useRef(null);
const previousValue = useRef(value);
const hasMounted = useRef(false);

useEffect(() => {
  if (!hasMounted.current) {
    hasMounted.current = true;
    return;                    // skip the effect on mount only
  }
  doSomethingOnUpdate();
});
```

If a value is rendered, it belongs in state. If it's only read by effects and handlers, a ref avoids a pointless render.

## Callback refs

Passing a **function** to `ref` gets you the node the moment it attaches, with no effect needed:

```javascript
<div ref={(node) => { if (node) measure(node); }} />
```

This matters because `useEffect` runs after the ref is populated but has no way to know *when* it changed — a node that appears conditionally won't retrigger a `[]` effect. A callback ref fires exactly when the node attaches or detaches.

Watch the identity trap: an inline arrow is a new function each render, so React calls it with `null` and then the node again on every render. Wrap it in `useCallback` if that's a problem.

**React 19** adds cleanup functions to callback refs, which removes the `null` dance:

```javascript
<div ref={(node) => {
  const observer = new ResizeObserver(handleResize);
  observer.observe(node);
  return () => observer.disconnect();   // React 19+
}} />
```

## Refs to a list of elements

There's no `useRef` per array item. Store a map:

```javascript
function List({ items }) {
  const itemRefs = useRef(new Map());

  const scrollTo = (id) => {
    itemRefs.current.get(id)?.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth' });
  };

  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map((item) => (
        <li
          key={item.id}
          ref={(node) => {
            if (node) itemRefs.current.set(item.id, node);
            else itemRefs.current.delete(item.id);   // clean up on unmount
          }}
        >
          {item.label}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

The `else` branch matters — without it the map leaks references to removed DOM nodes.

## Exposing an imperative API

Sometimes a parent genuinely needs to command a child: focus this, scroll to that, play, reset. Exposing the raw DOM node gives the parent far more power than it needs, so narrow it with `useImperativeHandle`:

```javascript
function FancyInput({ ref }) {          // React 19: ref is a normal prop
  const inputRef = useRef(null);

  useImperativeHandle(ref, () => ({
    focus: () => inputRef.current.focus(),
    clear: () => { inputRef.current.value = ''; },
  }), []);

  return <input ref={inputRef} />;
}

// Parent
const fancyRef = useRef(null);
<FancyInput ref={fancyRef} />
fancyRef.current.focus();
```

Pre-React-19 the same component needs `forwardRef`:

```javascript
const FancyInput = forwardRef(function FancyInput(props, ref) { /* ... */ });
```

**In React 19, `ref` is an ordinary prop and `forwardRef` is deprecated.** Existing code keeps working, but new components should just destructure `ref`.

Use an imperative handle sparingly — it's an escape from one-way data flow. Reach for it when the action genuinely has no state representation (focus, scroll, media playback), not to avoid lifting state.

## Why a ref can't be a dependency

```javascript
useEffect(() => {
  console.log(ref.current);
}, [ref]);          // never re-fires: `ref` is the same object forever
```

The ref object's identity never changes, so it's useless as a dependency — and `ref.current` isn't tracked at all, since mutating it doesn't notify React. If you need to react to a node appearing, use a callback ref or put the node in state.

